"Model. Color: the blackbody (Planck) spectrum of each temperature, its color through the CIE {#} color matching functions (demos/lib/bbcolor.js), and its peak from Wien's law, λ = b/T. Real stars are not perfect blackbodies. Lines: following Cecilia Payne ({#}), a line's strength is taken to follow the number of atoms able to make it: the element's abundance (Asplund and others {#}) times the share of its atoms in the right ionization stage (the Saha equation, ionization energies from NIST) times the share of those on the line's lower level (the Boltzmann equation). Partition functions are held at the statistical weight of each stage's ground term, an approximation that matters most for iron. The electron pressure is held fixed ({#} N/m², the round value of Carroll and Ostlie §{#}, unless you change it); in real stars it changes with temperature and surface gravity. Titanium oxide: chemical equilibrium of H, He, C, O and Ti with the molecules H₂, CO, OH, H₂O, TiO and TiO₂ from NIST-JANAF data at a gas pressure of {#} × {#}⁴ N/m² (the model of demos/spectra.html). The spectrum is drawn: each line's depth is set by its number of absorbers compared with that line's own largest number (Payne plotted each element against its own maximum), on a scale that saturates; it is not a radiative transfer calculation, and real line depths also depend on the continuum opacity (mostly H⁻ in cool stars). Line wavelengths in air from NIST; lower levels: H n = {#} ({#} eV), He I {#}³P ({#} eV), He II n = {#} ({#} eV); Ca II K and H, Ca I {#} nm, Na I D and the Fe I lines are counted from their ground stages. Spectral classes by temperature from OpenStax Astronomy 2e, Table {#}. Star temperatures in the setups are the lesson's round values (Betelgeuse about {#} K, Rigel about {#} K), the Sun's is the IAU nominal {#} K.",
